{% extends "base.html.jinja" %}
{% import "macros.jinja" as m %}

{% block title %}Request or correct a record | Significance{% endblock %}
{% block topline_meta %}Suggest a claim or correct an existing record{% endblock %}

{#
  Two actions, no backend. The site is static and stays static: a form posting
  somewhere would need a server, a store, and a policy about what happens to
  what people type into it, none of which this project has. A GitHub issue and
  an email are both places where the request is visible to the person making it
  and can be withdrawn by them.

  The GitHub link carries `?template=`, which opens the form at
  .github/ISSUE_TEMPLATE/record-request.yml with its three fields already
  labelled. The mailto carries the same three fields as a body, so somebody who
  will not open a GitHub account is not asked to answer a different set of
  questions.
#}
{% set repo = site.get('repository_url') %}
{% set template_name = site.get('record_request_template', 'record-request.yml') %}
{% set issue_url = (repo ~ '/issues/new?template=' ~ template_name) if repo else None %}
{% set contact = site.get('contact_email')|safe_email %}
{% set mail_subject = 'Record request' %}
{% set mail_body -%}
Link to the claim or Significance record:

Your role (author / reader / other):

Optional — what should change or receive attention:

{% endset %}

{% block content %}

<section class="band">
  <p class="lbl">Request or correct a record</p>
  <h1 class="claim-text">Request a record or suggest a correction.</h1>
  <p class="prose">
    Use a GitHub issue to suggest a mathematical claim for Significance or
    correct a page that is already here. Authors and readers can both submit.
    Start with a link and your role; add a short explanation if you can.
    You do not need to prepare a record or edit YAML.
  </p>
  {% if issue_url|safe_href %}<p><a class="button" href="{{ issue_url|safe_href }}">Open the request or correction form on GitHub</a></p>{% endif %}
  <p class="meta">GitHub issues are public. Keep private correspondence and personal information out of the form.</p>
</section>

<section class="band">
  <p class="lbl">How to submit on GitHub</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Open the form above. Sign in to GitHub or create an account if prompted.</li>
    <li>Give the issue a short title, such as “Record request: Erdős problem 3” or “Correction: source link on [record name].”</li>
    <li>Paste the link to the paper, repository, announcement, or existing Significance record.</li>
    <li>Select your role: author, reader, or other.</li>
    <li>Optionally explain what you want added or corrected. For a correction, identify the section or sentence, suggest the change, and link supporting sources.</li>
    <li>Choose <strong>Submit new issue</strong>. Return to the issue to answer questions or add sources in the comments.</li>
  </ol>
  <p class="prose">One issue should cover one claim or one focused correction. If you do not know what needs attention, leave the final field blank.</p>
</section>

<section class="band">
  <p class="lbl">What happens next</p>
  <ol>
    <li>A maintainer reads the issue, checks the sources, and asks for missing context when needed.</li>
    <li>For a new record, the maintainer applies the publication rules below and prepares a draft of the claim, source version, existing evidence, and open questions. For a correction, they compare the suggested change with the existing record and its sources.</li>
    <li>The proposed record or update goes through a pull request, validation, and human review. Attributed findings stay limited to the source version and scope examined.</li>
    <li>An accepted change is merged and published in the next successful site deployment. The issue can link to the change; the record keeps a history of updates.</li>
  </ol>
  <p class="prose">Opening an issue does not automatically publish a record, assign a reviewer, or turn a comment into evidence. A maintainer may close a request without making a record change.</p>
</section>

{% if site.get('request_turnaround') %}
<section class="band">
  <p class="lbl">Reply timing</p>
  <p class="prose">
    Requests are answered within {{ site.request_turnaround }}. That is a
    promise about the reply, not about the record: the answer may well be that
    no record will be created, and it will say why. A request that goes
    nowhere is closed without a public judgement attached to it.
  </p>
</section>
{% endif %}

<section class="band">
  <p class="lbl">Publication and author participation</p>
  <p class="prose">
    Widely circulating public claims from organisations or major announcements
    may receive editorial records compiled entirely from public sources. The
    page says plainly that its authors did not participate unless they did.
  </p>
  <p class="prose">
    Ordinary claims by individual living authors are published through an
    author request or opt-in. A reader may suggest one; maintainers contact the
    author privately before publication. If the author declines, no record is
    filed and no decline list is kept.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="band">
  <p class="lbl">What to include</p>
  <dl class="facts">
    <dt>Required: link</dt>
    <dd>A link to where it actually lives (paper, preprint, repository, announcement, post). The most stable one, if it is in several places.</dd>
    <dt>Required: your role</dt>
    <dd>Author, reader, or something else. This changes what happens next rather than how the request is ranked: an author can attest to their own work, and a reader asking about someone else's claim starts the contact step above.</dd>
    <dt>Optional: request or correction</dt>
    <dd>A correction, a theorem that matters, or a step you want examined. “Not sure” and a blank answer are both valid.</dd>
  </dl>
</section>

{% if not issue_url %}
<section class="band">
  <p class="lbl">GitHub submission</p>
  <p class="prose">
    The GitHub submission link is unavailable for this build.
  </p>
</section>
{% endif %}

{% if links.get('tasks') %}
<section class="band">
  <p class="lbl">Want to check a specific step instead?</p>
  <p class="prose">Go to <a href="{{ links.tasks }}">Open checks</a>, choose a task, and use its question, partial-attempt, or attestation link. A response should describe the exact source, what you checked, and what you found. Use this request form when a record is missing or an existing page needs correcting.</p>
</section>
{% endif %}

<section class="band">
  <p class="lbl">Already received a draft from Significance?</p>
  <p class="prose">
    Reply directly to that message with “accurate,” ordinary prose corrections,
    “keep this private,” or “do not mark this author-confirmed.” No account,
    schema, or separate form is required. Silence is never represented as
    approval.
  </p>
</section>

{% if contact %}
<section class="band">
  <p class="lbl">Send by email</p>
  <p class="prose">
    <a href="mailto:{{ contact }}?subject={{ mail_subject|urlencode }}&amp;body={{ mail_body|urlencode }}">Email the same three fields</a>,
    already templated in the message body. No account needed.
  </p>
</section>
{% endif %}

<section class="band what-this-does-not-establish">
  <p class="lbl">What filing a request does not do</p>
  <ul>
    <li>It does not commit anyone to creating a record or finding a reviewer.</li>
    <li>A request that goes nowhere is closed without a public judgement attached to it.</li>
    <li>A record, if one is created, still asserts nothing about whether the mathematics is right.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

{% endblock %}
